## Getting used to CPAP

- wear the mask for short periods while awake, such as watching TV
- make sure the mask fits well without being too tight
- clean the mask and tubing regularly
- contact your sleep clinic if you have problems. Do not just give up

## Travel

Take your CPAP machine on holiday. It usually counts as medical equipment on flights. Check with your airline and take a letter from your clinic.

## Good sleep habits

Keep regular sleep times, avoid alcohol and heavy meals late in the evening, and keep your bedroom cool and dark.

## Driving

You must tell the **DVLA** if you have OSA that causes **excessive sleepiness** that affects your driving. You must **not drive** until your symptoms are under control. Most people can drive again once treatment is working. Different rules apply to lorry and bus drivers.

Never drive if you feel sleepy.

## Support

The **Sleep Apnoea Trust** supports people with OSA and their partners.
